The attached image is a Western blot (Figure 7D from the PDF) illustrating the effect of Nano-11 and LPS on IL-1β processing and secretion in dendritic cells. It shows that pro-IL-1β (around 30kD) is present in cell lysates, particularly when primed with LPS. The active, cleaved IL-1β (approximately 17kD) is predominantly found in the supernatant, with significantly increased levels when cells are stimulated with Nano-11, especially after LPS priming. This confirms that Nano-11 induces the cleavage and secretion of active IL-1β, a crucial pro-inflammatory cytokine, which ELISA cannot distinguish from the inactive precursor.

The image demonstrates Nano-11's ability to induce the secretion of active IL-1β, a cytokine involved in immune activation. In cancer research, this property could be leveraged for immunotherapy or vaccine development. Nano-11 could serve as an adjuvant in therapeutic cancer vaccines to prime robust anti-tumor immune responses, utilizing IL-1β's role in promoting T-cell activation and immune cell recruitment. By locally enhancing IL-1β, Nano-11 might help overcome immune suppression in the tumor microenvironment, although careful consideration of IL-1β's dual role in tumor progression versus suppression is necessary for specific cancer types.